1
00:00:01,700 --> 00:00:04,693
Hi everyone, welcome back to Build with
Treesa!

2
00:00:04,713 --> 00:00:10,811
If you are starting with UiPath Agent
Builder, concepts like prompts, tools,
context, memory,

3
00:00:10,831 --> 00:00:15,728
escalations, guardrails and evaluations
can feel like a lot at first.

4
00:00:15,748 --> 00:00:20,451
So in this video, I'm going to simplify
the complete Agent Builder journey.

5
00:00:20,471 --> 00:00:25,198
We'll start with the basic building blocks
of an agent, then see how we give it data,

6
00:00:25,218 --> 00:00:30,675
instructions, knowledge and tools, and
finally how we debug, evaluate and deploy
it.

7
00:00:30,695 --> 00:00:37,071
By the end of this video, you should have
a clear mental model of how an agent is
actually built in UiPath.

8
00:00:38,621 --> 00:00:40,501
Let's start with the big picture.

9
00:00:40,521 --> 00:00:46,851
An agent needs instructions, knowledge,
capabilities and a way to involve humans
when required.

10
00:00:46,871 --> 00:00:48,520
First, we have the Prompt.

11
00:00:48,540 --> 00:00:54,523
The prompt defines the agent's role, goal,
instructions, constraints and expected
behaviour.

12
00:00:54,543 --> 00:00:55,849
Next are Tools.

13
00:00:55,869 --> 00:00:59,334
Tools allow the agent to interact with the
outside world.

14
00:00:59,354 --> 00:01:04,934
Instead of only generating text, the agent
can call an API, run an automation,

15
00:01:04,954 --> 00:01:10,235
execute an activity, invoke another agent
or connect through an MCP server.

16
00:01:10,255 --> 00:01:11,788
Then we have Context.

17
00:01:11,808 --> 00:01:17,003
Context gives the agent access to relevant
enterprise knowledge while it is
reasoning.

18
00:01:17,023 --> 00:01:18,525
And we have Escalations,

19
00:01:18,545 --> 00:01:24,377
which allow the agent to involve a human
when approval, review or assistance is
required.

20
00:01:24,397 --> 00:01:26,961
You'll also see Memory in Agent Builder.

21
00:01:26,981 --> 00:01:34,345
Memory allows the agent to learn from
previous executions and reuse relevant
past experience in future runs.

22
00:01:34,365 --> 00:01:36,346
So a simple mental model is:

23
00:01:36,366 --> 00:01:38,425
Prompt tells the agent what to do.

24
00:01:38,445 --> 00:01:40,628
Context tells it what it needs to know.

25
00:01:40,648 --> 00:01:43,203
Tools give it the ability to take action.

26
00:01:43,223 --> 00:01:46,217
Memory helps it learn from previous
experiences.

27
00:01:46,237 --> 00:01:49,191
And escalations tell it when to involve a
human.

28
00:01:50,741 --> 00:01:53,190
Now, where do we actually build all of
this?

29
00:01:53,210 --> 00:01:59,371
UiPath Agent Builder gives us two ways to
work with an agent: Form View and Canvas
View.

30
00:01:59,391 --> 00:02:02,953
Form View gives us a structured
configuration experience.

31
00:02:02,973 --> 00:02:07,897
Canvas View gives us a visual, node-based
representation of the agent.

32
00:02:07,917 --> 00:02:12,349
The important thing to understand is that
these are not two different agents.

33
00:02:12,369 --> 00:02:15,606
They represent the same underlying agent
definition.

34
00:02:15,626 --> 00:02:19,705
If you change something in one view, it
remains synchronized with the other.

35
00:02:19,725 --> 00:02:25,615
Inside the workspace, we also have areas
such as Project Explorer, Data Manager,
Errors,

36
00:02:25,635 --> 00:02:29,534
Properties, Development options and
Deployment Configuration.

37
00:02:29,554 --> 00:02:34,302
At the bottom, we have some very useful
capabilities that we'll come back to
later:

38
00:02:34,322 --> 00:02:37,277
Execution Trail, History and Evaluations.

39
00:02:38,827 --> 00:02:43,089
Before making the agent intelligent, we
should first define its contract.

40
00:02:43,109 --> 00:02:45,476
What information does the agent receive?

41
00:02:45,496 --> 00:02:47,547
And what information should it return?

42
00:02:47,567 --> 00:02:49,861
That is where Data Manager comes in.

43
00:02:49,881 --> 00:02:52,927
Imagine we are creating an IT support
agent.

44
00:02:52,947 --> 00:02:59,300
Inputs could include: a customer ID, a
ticket description, a priority, or some
document text.

45
00:02:59,320 --> 00:03:05,799
The outputs might be: a classification, a
summary, a recommended action, or a final
resolution.

46
00:03:05,819 --> 00:03:11,133
So the flow becomes: application or
workflow, to input arguments, to the
agent,

47
00:03:11,153 --> 00:03:15,714
to output arguments, and finally to the
next application or process.

48
00:03:15,734 --> 00:03:20,461
This is important because an agent
shouldn't just return random free-form
text

49
00:03:20,481 --> 00:03:23,486
when another system needs to consume the
result.

50
00:03:23,506 --> 00:03:29,815
Clearly defined inputs and outputs make
the agent much easier to integrate into
real automation.

51
00:03:31,365 --> 00:03:34,856
Now we come to one of the most important
parts: Prompts.

52
00:03:34,876 --> 00:03:39,231
Think of the system prompt as the
operating instructions for your agent.

53
00:03:39,251 --> 00:03:40,473
It should explain:

54
00:03:40,493 --> 00:03:44,544
Who are you? What are you trying to
achieve? What constraints should you
follow?

55
00:03:44,564 --> 00:03:49,982
When should you use a tool? When should
you retrieve context? When should you
escalate to a human?

56
00:03:50,002 --> 00:03:52,317
And what should the final response look
like?

57
00:03:52,337 --> 00:03:57,943
Then we have the user prompt, which
provides the actual task for that
particular execution.

58
00:03:57,963 --> 00:04:02,173
For example: Analyse this incident and
recommend the next action.

59
00:04:02,193 --> 00:04:06,839
Agent Builder also allows us to reference
different capabilities from the prompt.

60
00:04:06,859 --> 00:04:11,803
So instead of writing isolated
instructions, we can connect our prompt to
inputs,

61
00:04:11,823 --> 00:04:15,488
tools, context, memory, escalations and
outputs.

62
00:04:15,508 --> 00:04:19,493
And this is an important
prompt-engineering principle for agents:

63
00:04:19,513 --> 00:04:22,077
Don't only tell the agent what it should
achieve.

64
00:04:22,097 --> 00:04:25,803
Tell it when and how it should use the
capabilities available to it.

65
00:04:27,353 --> 00:04:32,084
This is where an AI agent becomes much
more useful than a normal chatbot.

66
00:04:32,104 --> 00:04:35,768
Without tools, the model can reason and
generate an answer.

67
00:04:35,788 --> 00:04:38,280
With tools, it can actually do something.

68
00:04:38,300 --> 00:04:44,851
In UiPath, tools can include Integration
Service activities, published automations,
APIs,

69
00:04:44,871 --> 00:04:49,858
custom integrations, other agents,
functions, MCP servers and more.

70
00:04:49,878 --> 00:04:52,413
The basic tool execution loop is simple.

71
00:04:52,433 --> 00:04:55,574
First, the agent decides which tool is
required.

72
00:04:55,594 --> 00:04:57,332
Then it prepares the inputs.

73
00:04:57,352 --> 00:04:58,885
It executes the tool.

74
00:04:58,905 --> 00:05:00,371
It reads the result.

75
00:05:00,391 --> 00:05:03,587
And based on that result, it decides what
to do next.

76
00:05:03,607 --> 00:05:09,234
For example, imagine an employee asks: Can
you check the status of my laptop request?

77
00:05:09,254 --> 00:05:13,768
The agent may decide that it needs to call
an asset-management API.

78
00:05:13,788 --> 00:05:15,705
It passes the employee ID.

79
00:05:15,725 --> 00:05:17,978
The tool returns the request status.

80
00:05:17,998 --> 00:05:22,168
The agent interprets that result and then
responds to the employee.

81
00:05:22,188 --> 00:05:26,258
One thing people often underestimate here
is the tool description.

82
00:05:26,278 --> 00:05:30,305
A tool description isn't only
documentation for developers.

83
00:05:30,325 --> 00:05:34,368
It helps the agent understand what the
tool does and when it should use it.

84
00:05:34,388 --> 00:05:38,905
So clear tool names, descriptions, inputs
and outputs matter a lot.

85
00:05:40,455 --> 00:05:42,267
Tools help the agent act.

86
00:05:42,287 --> 00:05:44,253
Context helps the agent know.

87
00:05:44,273 --> 00:05:49,175
Imagine an HR agent answering questions
about company leave policies.

88
00:05:49,195 --> 00:05:53,916
We don't want it to rely only on what the
underlying LLM already knows.

89
00:05:53,936 --> 00:05:56,710
We want it to use our actual company
policy.

90
00:05:56,730 --> 00:06:02,143
Through Context Grounding, the agent can
retrieve relevant enterprise knowledge
while reasoning.

91
00:06:02,163 --> 00:06:05,909
That knowledge might come from policies,
product documentation,

92
00:06:05,929 --> 00:06:10,674
process documentation, internal knowledge
bases or business information.

93
00:06:10,694 --> 00:06:14,984
The flow is essentially: user request,
retrieve relevant context,

94
00:06:15,004 --> 00:06:18,512
use enterprise knowledge, and then make a
grounded decision.

95
00:06:18,532 --> 00:06:21,253
So one very simple rule to remember is:

96
00:06:21,273 --> 00:06:24,054
Better context leads to better decisions.

97
00:06:25,604 --> 00:06:28,790
Context tells the agent what is relevant
right now.

98
00:06:28,810 --> 00:06:31,923
Memory allows it to benefit from what
happened before.

99
00:06:31,943 --> 00:06:37,601
UiPath currently provides concepts such as
episodic memory and escalation memory.

100
00:06:37,621 --> 00:06:41,992
Episodic Memory helps the agent remember
similar previous cases,

101
00:06:42,012 --> 00:06:45,285
including inputs, outputs, outcomes and
feedback.

102
00:06:45,305 --> 00:06:50,744
Suppose an agent previously handled a
particular type of support request
successfully.

103
00:06:50,764 --> 00:06:56,966
When it encounters a similar request
later, that previous experience can help
guide the new execution.

104
00:06:56,986 --> 00:07:00,010
Escalation Memory is particularly
interesting.

105
00:07:00,030 --> 00:07:05,614
Imagine an agent previously encountered an
uncertain case and escalated it to a
human.

106
00:07:05,634 --> 00:07:07,165
The human resolved it.

107
00:07:07,185 --> 00:07:11,340
If the agent later encounters a
sufficiently similar situation,

108
00:07:11,360 --> 00:07:15,345
it can use that previous human decision as
useful context.

109
00:07:15,365 --> 00:07:20,769
Memories are organised inside Memory
Spaces, and these can be shared across
agents.

110
00:07:20,789 --> 00:07:22,768
The broader idea is powerful:

111
00:07:22,788 --> 00:07:27,825
Instead of treating every execution as if
the system has never seen anything before,

112
00:07:27,845 --> 00:07:31,012
the agent can learn from relevant past
experiences.

113
00:07:32,562 --> 00:07:36,310
But an agent should not always make every
decision autonomously.

114
00:07:36,330 --> 00:07:39,845
There are situations where a human should
remain involved.

115
00:07:39,865 --> 00:07:41,845
That is where Escalations come in.

116
00:07:41,865 --> 00:07:47,087
An agent might escalate for: approval,
review, correction, or exception handling.

117
00:07:47,107 --> 00:07:51,149
For example, imagine an agent processing
refund requests.

118
00:07:51,169 --> 00:07:56,127
If the refund is below a certain
threshold, perhaps it can continue
automatically.

119
00:07:56,147 --> 00:08:01,322
But if the amount is high, we may want a
human to approve it before the next action
happens.

120
00:08:01,342 --> 00:08:04,914
This is an important concept in agentic
automation.

121
00:08:04,934 --> 00:08:07,938
Human-in-the-loop doesn't mean the
automation failed.

122
00:08:07,958 --> 00:08:12,687
It means we intentionally designed the
system so that AI handles what it can,

123
00:08:12,707 --> 00:08:16,045
while humans retain control over important
decisions.

124
00:08:17,595 --> 00:08:21,125
Once agents can take actions, we also need
boundaries.

125
00:08:21,145 --> 00:08:23,600
That is where Guardrails become important.

126
00:08:23,620 --> 00:08:27,094
Before allowing a tool to execute, we
might want to check:

127
00:08:27,114 --> 00:08:32,122
Is this action allowed? Are the inputs
valid? Is sensitive information involved?

128
00:08:32,142 --> 00:08:36,283
Does this require approval? Should this
action be logged or monitored?

129
00:08:36,303 --> 00:08:39,827
If the request passes the guardrail, the
tool can execute.

130
00:08:39,847 --> 00:08:43,053
If it doesn't, we can block the action or
escalate it.

131
00:08:43,073 --> 00:08:47,821
This is especially important when an agent
can update systems, send messages,

132
00:08:47,841 --> 00:08:50,737
change records or trigger business
processes.

133
00:08:50,757 --> 00:08:54,102
Agent autonomy should never mean unlimited
access.

134
00:08:54,122 --> 00:08:57,614
It should mean autonomy inside clearly
defined boundaries.

135
00:08:59,164 --> 00:09:02,643
Behind the agent is the model that powers
its reasoning.

136
00:09:02,663 --> 00:09:10,061
Agent Builder gives us controls such as
the model itself, temperature, maximum
tokens and maximum iterations.

137
00:09:10,081 --> 00:09:15,025
Temperature controls how deterministic or
creative the generation should be.

138
00:09:15,045 --> 00:09:19,095
For many business processes, lower
temperature is usually useful

139
00:09:19,115 --> 00:09:22,900
because we want consistent behaviour
rather than creativity.

140
00:09:22,920 --> 00:09:27,644
Maximum tokens controls how much the model
can generate in a response.

141
00:09:27,664 --> 00:09:32,725
And maximum iterations controls how many
reasoning loops the agent can go through.

142
00:09:32,745 --> 00:09:34,268
That loop might look like:

143
00:09:34,288 --> 00:09:39,148
Think. Use a tool or retrieve context.
Observe the result. Think again.

144
00:09:39,168 --> 00:09:41,866
And continue until the task is completed.

145
00:09:41,886 --> 00:09:45,913
But more iterations do not automatically
mean a better agent.

146
00:09:45,933 --> 00:09:50,020
We want enough room for reasoning without
allowing unnecessary loops.

147
00:09:51,570 --> 00:09:54,702
Finally, building the agent is only the
beginning.

148
00:09:54,722 --> 00:09:58,600
Before putting it into production, we need
to understand how it behaves.

149
00:09:58,620 --> 00:10:01,886
That's where the Execution Trail becomes
extremely useful.

150
00:10:01,906 --> 00:10:06,835
Instead of only looking at the final
answer, we can inspect what happened
during the run.

151
00:10:06,855 --> 00:10:12,241
What tool did the agent call? What inputs
did it send? What context did it retrieve?

152
00:10:12,261 --> 00:10:15,000
What output came back? Where did an error
happen?

153
00:10:15,020 --> 00:10:18,588
We can also use History to look at
previous executions.

154
00:10:18,608 --> 00:10:20,374
Then we have Evaluations.

155
00:10:20,394 --> 00:10:23,739
This is how we move from: It worked when I
tested it once,

156
00:10:23,759 --> 00:10:29,023
to: We have evidence that this agent
behaves consistently across different
scenarios.

157
00:10:29,043 --> 00:10:34,165
We can create evaluation sets, define
expected outcomes, run multiple tests

158
00:10:34,185 --> 00:10:38,149
and improve the prompts, tools and context
based on the results.

159
00:10:38,169 --> 00:10:44,001
Once we are satisfied, we can publish and
deploy the agent, observe how it behaves
in production,

160
00:10:44,021 --> 00:10:46,680
collect feedback and continue improving
it.

161
00:10:48,231 --> 00:10:53,775
So if you want one checklist for building
an agent in UiPath, think about it in this
order:

162
00:10:53,795 --> 00:10:54,992
Define the goal.

163
00:10:55,012 --> 00:10:57,010
Define your inputs and outputs.

164
00:10:57,030 --> 00:10:58,466
Write clear prompts.

165
00:10:58,486 --> 00:10:59,914
Configure the model.

166
00:10:59,934 --> 00:11:01,305
Add the right tools.

167
00:11:01,325 --> 00:11:03,087
Provide the right context.

168
00:11:03,107 --> 00:11:05,144
Use memory where it adds value.

169
00:11:05,164 --> 00:11:07,002
Define escalation paths.

170
00:11:07,022 --> 00:11:08,340
Add guardrails.

171
00:11:08,360 --> 00:11:09,955
Debug the execution.

172
00:11:09,975 --> 00:11:11,523
Evaluate the behaviour.

173
00:11:11,543 --> 00:11:13,832
Then publish, observe and improve.

174
00:11:13,852 --> 00:11:17,602
Because a good agent is not simply an
agent that works once.

175
00:11:17,622 --> 00:11:23,526
A good agent is one that behaves reliably
across many different real-world
situations.

176
00:11:23,546 --> 00:11:26,919
If you found this useful, save the Agent
Builder cheat sheet

177
00:11:26,939 --> 00:11:31,729
and share this video with someone who is
starting their journey with UiPath agents.

178
00:11:31,749 --> 00:11:38,825
And if you enjoy practical content around
UiPath, automation, AI agents and agentic
automation,

179
00:11:38,845 --> 00:11:40,806
subscribe to Build with Treesa.

180
00:11:40,826 --> 00:11:44,006
Thanks for watching, and I'll see you in
the next video!
